# Typed Manager terminal evidence capsules

`build_manager_capsule.py` is an offline, narrowly typed validator/builder for source98 Manager
bridge success records. It requires the actual intentional SIGINT exit **130**. It never converts
that exit to zero and never modifies the run. The frozen `build_capsule.py` remains unchanged at
SHA-256 `e1c63334e87118c06e4ef5f75c2aa1dee3b4c8cda3a6a0602ce68f0a412e9fbd`; its ordinary successful
terminal-run validator continues to require exit0. This new type rejects unrecognized schemas,
failed or incomplete bridge gates, different exit codes, missing status/serving/rank receipts,
controller escalation, survivors, and inventory changes. It does not reinterpret failed runs.

The accepted controller schema is `muxpd-source98-controller-execution-v1`, mode `manager`, role
`raw` or `group`, clean source `98eac12dc2595a8742a81b58e96b899fed905194`. The actual controller field
is **`manager_runtime_binding`**, containing journal identity, plan ID, launch ID and Manager
process identity. There is no assumed `actual_manager_binding` field.

Validation checks the retained preparation and every original snapshot; actual root aliases
`plan.json` = `manager-state/plan-source.json` and `engine-args.json` = saved Manager configuration;
exact common helper CLI; identical prepared/launched source tables and Manager projection;
launch-source `reviewed_plan`; and retained dry-run stdout. Manager journal `plan_id` hashes bundle
registration in this installed implementation, so it is **not** treated as a hash of launch
arguments/configuration. Configuration is bound by the separate chain above.

The validator independently recomputes the journal binding digest and bundle-only plan ID, binds
all effective component events to the exact launch and captured descendant PID/start identities,
and requires all four worker startup readbacks to agree with the journal. The pinned existing
startup parser is re-executed from SHA-pinned source using compile/exec, only on retained log
bytes; it imports only stdlib, writes no bytecode cache and starts no service. Runtime-effectiveness must be false before, true live, and false stopped. Serving must
retain the original P128 prompt and HTTP200, one length-finished choice, eight completion tokens.

Cleanup requires exactly the controller's intentional SIGINT, no controller escalation, no
recorded survivors, and no still-live captured PID/start identity or captured process group,
including the separate API session. Both archived `npu-smi` snapshots must identify exactly four
empty physical 910B2 devices1,2,3,6. No new `npu-smi`, service, network, or NPU operation is made.

These are **bounded-shutdown/source-bridge** receipts. The source98 raw log contains native API
abort shutdown, force-killing of one remaining process, EngineCore SIGTERM, and resource-tracker
warnings for four semaphore and six shared-memory objects. Relevant lines are explicitly retained
in `terminal.native_shutdown_log_caveats`, as well as the complete original process log. The
Manager's recorded ten-second grace heuristic cannot establish internal worker signal choices.
Every terminal and capsule receipt sets `graceful_internal_drain_proven=false`. No wheel/release,
quality, universal determinism, or performance qualification is inferred.

## Archive contract

Manifest kind: `terminal-source98-manager-sigint-bridge`.
Manifest schema: `muxpd-manager-evidence-capsule-manifest-v1`.
Receipt schema: `muxpd-manager-evidence-capsule-receipt-v1`.
Member entries retain the existing `member`, original absolute `path`, `type`, `bytes`, `sha256`,
and symlink-target conventions. Existing terminal common fields remain available:
`run_path`, `label`, `controller`, `started_at`, `finished_at`, `exit_code=130`,
`controller_passed=true`, `source_repository`, `source_commit`, `source_status`.

All original files, logs, compiler cache artifacts and producer bytes under the explicit run are
hashed. The builder validates terminal state and the **complete** inventory before and after
compression, verifies compressed archive SHA and every archived member, and writes a successful
receipt only after those checks. Added files are rejected as well as deleted/changed files.
Directories are append-only; build failures retain `BUILD-FAILED.json` and do not produce a success
receipt. Original absolute-path custody checks are local; relocation needs an explicit mapping.

```bash
python build_manager_capsule.py validate --run RUN
python build_manager_capsule.py freeze --run RUN --capsule-id SAFE-ID --output NEW-MANIFEST.json
python build_manager_capsule.py build --manifest NEW-MANIFEST.json --output NEW-CAPSULE-DIRECTORY
```

Focused CPU validation: `test_manager_capsule.py`,14 tests; current log
`manager-capsule-cpu-tests-v3.log`; Ruff log `manager-capsule-ruff-v2.log`. Fixtures preserve
actual130 and demonstrate the original builder still rejects passing130; reject alternate exits,
missing terminal gates, false/live/stopped status errors, escalation/survivors, still-live separate
session, incomplete startup/serving, physical inventory changes, and added files before/after build.
The initial test run's malformed-timestamp exception is preserved in
`manager-capsule-cpu-tests-v1.log`; the validator now rejects that case explicitly with ValueError.

The initial validator importlib attempt generated one new startup-helper bytecode cache in each
terminal run. Those two generated files were preserved outside the runs with their original paths,
hashes and timestamps, then only those new files were removed to restore the original inventories.
See `manager-validator-bytecode-side-effect-v1/receipt.json`. No existing result, producer or
preexisting bytecode was changed. A full inventory-before/after validation fixture now prevents
this side effect. This restoration is disclosed; it is not a claim that no post-run tool touched
the directories. No capsule was frozen before the correction.

Final validation additionally pins both Manager/helper producer files and the frozen21-file
source manifest, and binds the prepared/controller environment to the actual launch pre-CLI
environment. Parent HCCL/BATCH settings are absent; the explicit profile is required to select
HCCLtrue in the post-initialization worker readbacks. Source PYTHONPATH and isolated Manager state
path must agree with the actual run.
